**How the security goals are met:**

| Goal | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | AES-256-GCM. A reader of the storage sees only a random IV + ciphertext + 16-byte tag. The tag length is fixed (16 B), so the only leakable info is `plaintext.length + 16 + 12` bytes, i.e. approximate length. |
| **Owner / name isolation** | Each secret's GCM **associated data (AAD)** is `UTF-8(owner) ‖ 0x00 ‖ UTF-8(name)`. GCM binds the tag to the AAD, so a blob valid for `(owner A, name X)` will fail authentication when decrypted under `(owner B, …)` or `(owner A, name Y)`. A wrong master password yields a different AES key, so every tag check fails and `get` throws. |
| **Tamper detection** | GCM's authentication tag (128-bit) covers IV + ciphertext + AAD. Any bit flip, truncation, or wholesale copy of another secret's blob causes `subtle.decrypt` to throw, which `get` re-raises as an `Error`. |
| **Persistence across store recreations** | The PBKDF2 salt is written to a dedicated storage key on first use and read back on every subsequent `createSecretStore` call with the same password, reproducing the same AES key. |
| **No index to tamper with** | `list` reconstructs the name set by scanning `storage.keys()` with the owner's prefix, so there is no auxiliary metadata an attacker could corrupt to hide or inject secrets. |

